1
00:00:01,438 --> 00:00:08,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's this really common view nowadays that stream-entry is attainable without a major degree of restraint.

2
00:00:08,522 --> 00:00:14,446
[SPEAKER_00]: That, basically, you only need to keep the five precepts and, you know, live a moral life.

3
00:00:14,566 --> 00:00:16,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do the obvious bad stuff.

4
00:00:17,328 --> 00:00:26,353
[SPEAKER_00]: And that sense-restraint and sadbhāsī and all these things are for monks or for people who are striving higher than just stream-entry.

5
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:45,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, the proof for that, supposedly, is that people in the suttas, these lay people, were getting the right view in the Buddhist time, despite the fact that they were clearly living with their spouses and, you know, enjoying their wealth and all that stuff.

6
00:00:45,497 --> 00:00:55,741
[SPEAKER_00]: So... but what people fail to see is that there is a very big difference in terms of both what those people had, that we don't have,

7
00:00:56,597 --> 00:01:02,819
[SPEAKER_00]: And what... something that we have that they don't have, which is not necessarily a good thing.

8
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:23,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... even if you actually read carefully the descriptions that the suttas give of the, you know, the wider context within which those attainments of the right view were happening for those people, you will see, first of all, that many of them had never met the Buddha,

9
00:01:24,699 --> 00:01:34,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they were talking to him for the first time, and second of all, they were... they heard one discourse, and they got the right view after that.

10
00:01:34,588 --> 00:01:34,888
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

11
00:01:36,579 --> 00:01:47,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, you also see what what is exactly the suttas themselves say, what is exactly the thing that allowed the insight of the Four Noble Truths to take root in their minds.

12
00:01:47,907 --> 00:02:00,296
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's every single time it says the Buddha was giving them a talk on virtue, on the basic virtue, like living a moral life that leads to, you know, rewards in the next life and so on.

13
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:02,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he went on to describe

14
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:18,269
[SPEAKER_00]: The dangers of sensuality, like even of the next life, of anything that is... like the danger even in those heavenly rewards that you would get out of that... keeping the five precepts, for example.

15
00:02:18,749 --> 00:02:30,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they say that when their minds were actually withdrawn, that they were free from hindrances specifically, which mainly means sensual desire, then

16
00:02:31,808 --> 00:02:37,513
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha went on to teach the Four Noble Truths and then, you know, they understood it and became sotāpannas.

17
00:02:38,313 --> 00:02:48,181
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, people fail to see specifically and most importantly, what it means for the mind to be free from hindrances, as the suttas say.

18
00:02:48,841 --> 00:02:56,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So, fundamentally, one of the main wrong views today is that, you know, your mind is free from hindrances when you're

19
00:02:57,174 --> 00:03:03,058
[SPEAKER_00]: You happen not to be thinking about sensual objects at the moment, or you happen not to be angry, or whatever.

20
00:03:03,839 --> 00:03:05,860
[SPEAKER_00]: But the hindrances are much deeper than that.

21
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:10,223
[SPEAKER_00]: The hindrances are on the level of, like, your most fundamental state of being.

22
00:03:11,384 --> 00:03:26,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to put it concisely, it's inconceivable for a person who has any sort of, even remotely, like, a value, a view that sensuality is valuable,

23
00:03:27,749 --> 00:03:32,392
[SPEAKER_00]: to be free from the hindrance of sensual desire, because that is what the hindrance of sensual desire is.

24
00:03:32,932 --> 00:03:51,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if, at the moment, you are completely focused on your bodily sensations, and you're not thinking, supposedly, if you are capable of valuing sensual objects, if they were to arise, regardless of whether they have arisen at the moment, your mind is not free from sensual desire at that moment.

25
00:03:52,063 --> 00:03:56,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... the hindrance is still there, regardless of whatever

26
00:03:56,988 --> 00:04:00,110
[SPEAKER_00]: special meditation pleasure you might be having.

27
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:02,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, by the way, was not... it was not even a factor.

28
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:13,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, these meditation techniques were not even a factor in, you know, the procedure leading up to that attainment of the right view for those lay people back in the day.

29
00:04:13,656 --> 00:04:19,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they were not doing any of that at all, and neither there nor before.

30
00:04:20,797 --> 00:04:26,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Evidenced by the fact that they were following... many of them were following the views of other teachers who didn't even teach meditation.

31
00:04:27,059 --> 00:04:40,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... the... the thing... the point I'm trying to make is the thing that actually withdraws your mind from the hindrances is actual dispassion towards sensuality.

32
00:04:40,764 --> 00:04:49,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for those people, since their minds were naturally, you could say, pure, eh...

33
00:04:50,168 --> 00:05:06,876
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't need as much of, you know, a discrete separate effort beforehand to really develop that dispassion to the point where their minds could really just see sensual pleasure for what they are, simply on account of hearing a discourse.

34
00:05:07,596 --> 00:05:15,860
[SPEAKER_00]: But for most of us today, we're so far down the deep, the rabbit hole of sensuality that, you know, even just

35
00:05:17,708 --> 00:05:20,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Years of practice is not necessarily enough.

36
00:05:20,210 --> 00:05:34,001
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, even becoming a monk is not even a guarantee that your mind will actually be withdrawn from sensuality, because the Buddha said in another sutta that... We'll try to link the relevant suttas in the comment section.

37
00:05:35,002 --> 00:05:40,986
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that it doesn't matter, like, how many external observances a person might adhere to.

38
00:05:42,327 --> 00:05:45,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, if internally, there is still that value that I mentioned, that

39
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:56,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning towards the possibility of sensuality anytime in the future, even if it's in the next life, the mind is not free from sensual desire.

40
00:05:56,803 --> 00:06:05,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And so... And because of that, it won't actually be able to give rise to true knowledge, simply on account of that.

41
00:06:05,507 --> 00:06:14,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he gave a simile that it's like two wet sticks that you try to use them to make a fire, but obviously, because they're wet, you know, you can't even light a spark with them.

42
00:06:15,011 --> 00:06:18,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you actually need to dry out the sticks first and then you can light the fire.

43
00:06:18,973 --> 00:06:23,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, lighting the fire in that case refers to, you know, the getting the right view pretty much.

44
00:06:24,135 --> 00:06:33,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all that is just pointing to the fact that the mind needs to be internally dispassionate towards sensuality in order to

45
00:06:34,607 --> 00:06:35,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, understand the dhamma.

46
00:06:35,687 --> 00:06:51,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, basically, the value... the valuing of sensuality is like the most... the coarsest, the most... the most hindering way of not seeing the Second Noble Truth.

47
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:54,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everyone wants to be free from pain.

48
00:06:55,451 --> 00:07:04,174
[SPEAKER_00]: And to the degree that they still regard sensuality as a potential solution to that problem, they're not seeing the second Noble Truth.

49
00:07:04,855 --> 00:07:05,455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that simple.

50
00:07:06,075 --> 00:07:12,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any attempt to see it, even like hearing a discourse from the Buddha himself, won't really make a difference.

51
00:07:13,598 --> 00:07:24,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but obviously, you know, the argument that is made, and there's in fact another sutta where there's this layman who is

52
00:07:25,782 --> 00:07:28,023
[SPEAKER_00]: He's already a stream-enterer and he's drinking.

53
00:07:28,524 --> 00:07:33,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's breaking even the basic five precepts.

54
00:07:34,447 --> 00:07:45,874
[SPEAKER_00]: People were criticizing him and the Buddha explained to them that even though he's drinking, he's still a stream-enterer because he actually understood the Four Noble Truths.

55
00:07:45,894 --> 00:07:55,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, there is a truth in the sense that once you understand the dhamma,

56
00:07:55,817 --> 00:07:59,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You can afford to not be restrained.

57
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:21,979
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is that before that, if you allow yourself the possibility to not just engage, but value sensuality, even if you're not engaging with it, for example, as a monk, you're limited from engaging with sensuality, but internally, you can very much be valuing it.

58
00:08:22,379 --> 00:08:23,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that value is not given up,

59
00:08:26,004 --> 00:08:39,912
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be able to get the right view, which means your non-restraint is something very different, like, from what's happening internally, let's say, when a stream-enterer is not restrained.

60
00:08:40,273 --> 00:08:53,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a huge, immense difference between what's internally happening in a stream-enterer when they, say, break the five precepts, and what's happening

61
00:08:54,387 --> 00:08:55,967
[SPEAKER_00]: In a putujjana, who doesn't have the right view.

62
00:08:56,548 --> 00:09:08,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, like, it's... no matter how much a stream-enterer might stray away from the path, the fact is they see the path with, like, perfect certainty.

63
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:16,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they can re-establish that with which they acquired pretty much in a finger-snap, as an act of will.

64
00:09:17,152 --> 00:09:27,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But for whenever a pūtugina engages with sensuality, what they're doing is essentially like sinking deeper in a hole that they already don't know how to get out of.

65
00:09:28,538 --> 00:09:35,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what's happening when somebody who doesn't have the right view, before having the right view,

66
00:09:36,181 --> 00:09:57,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's actually another simile, where the developed mind, let's say of a stream-enterer, is compared to the great ocean, where if you were to dump even a big load of salt, it wouldn't really change anything, whereas the undeveloped mind is like a little glass of water, where even just a bit of salt is going to make a very noticeable difference.

67
00:09:57,772 --> 00:10:01,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that salt is basically like the external

68
00:10:09,819 --> 00:10:24,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, expanding the mind up to that great ocean, with the little glass of water that you start off with, is done through saying no to your desires.

69
00:10:24,730 --> 00:10:30,594
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not done through focusing on one object, which is basically taking you in the opposite direction, narrowing the mind down.

70
00:10:32,595 --> 00:10:41,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to your desires expands the mind, because the only reason why you feel that impulse to say yes by default, is because the mind is already tunnel-visioned towards those things.

71
00:10:41,738 --> 00:10:42,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already completely constricted.

72
00:10:43,679 --> 00:10:49,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, you don't even recognize that that doesn't have to be that way, because you've been living that way since forever.

73
00:10:51,002 --> 00:10:59,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you justify to yourself that you don't have to give it up, you're just justifying that little glass of water that you have, and not going beyond that.

74
00:11:01,430 --> 00:11:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: There's even one sutta where the Buddha says that the cause for the five hindrances is misconduct of body, speech and mind.

75
00:11:08,692 --> 00:11:13,233
[SPEAKER_00]: You will never find a sutta saying that it's the inability to focus on one object.

76
00:11:13,893 --> 00:11:21,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And the issue is not so much like the fact that your... let's say your virtue and your restraint is not perfect right off the bat.

77
00:11:21,915 --> 00:11:25,536
[SPEAKER_00]: The main problem is the view that you don't have to be restrained.

78
00:11:25,996 --> 00:11:31,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Because as the Buddha said, view... like the wrong view is the most pernicious of all things.

79
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:50,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Even worse than, you know, if there are every now and then slips in your behavior, but you were genuinely trying, the really bad thing is when you have a view that what you're doing is justified or is fine, that you don't have to be restrained because that's not in the contract when you're a layman.

80
00:11:52,146 --> 00:11:58,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing that people don't really see.

81
00:11:58,728 --> 00:11:59,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if, like,

82
00:12:01,112 --> 00:12:13,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you can get the right view as a layman, but what you have to differentiate is the state of a layman who doesn't have the right view and the state of a layman who does.

83
00:12:15,242 --> 00:12:27,090
[SPEAKER_00]: The process that those people underwent in, let's say, an hour or 45 minutes of talking to the Buddha, to the point where their minds became

84
00:12:28,058 --> 00:12:43,965
[SPEAKER_00]: internally withdrawn from the hindrances, that process today is what would take years for a person, because that's how much more pollution there is in the mind, due to previous habits and all that.

85
00:12:44,665 --> 00:12:52,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that additional baggage that we carry, that they didn't have back then.

86
00:12:55,106 --> 00:13:01,798
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, you don't even see people in the suttas struggling to get the right view, the way we do today.

87
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:05,365
[SPEAKER_00]: People who heard the Buddha for the first time,

88
00:13:06,219 --> 00:13:12,281
[SPEAKER_00]: developed real faith in the teaching, and yet they were unable, for some reason, to get the right view, the way it happens today.

89
00:13:12,721 --> 00:13:22,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Everyone back then who got the right view was, like, it was basically as soon as they recognized that the dhamma is a true way, they got the right view.

90
00:13:22,504 --> 00:13:27,946
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as they acknowledged that, that they sort of converted to the teaching, they got the right view.

91
00:13:30,027 --> 00:13:32,788
[SPEAKER_00]: The other people who didn't, you can see, like, there was, for example,

92
00:13:33,463 --> 00:13:48,853
[SPEAKER_00]: This King Pasenadi, who... he did respect the Buddha, he gave offerings and stuff, but he was still agreeing to some extent with the views of other teachers, which means he hadn't actually accepted fully the rightness of the dhamma.

93
00:13:48,913 --> 00:13:53,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's one of the people who didn't get the right view, even though he talked to the Buddha many times.

94
00:13:53,235 --> 00:14:00,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the point I'm making is you don't see like these... the way you see today, like people who are genuinely devoted

95
00:14:01,423 --> 00:14:14,611
[SPEAKER_00]: To the dhamma, they don't have any sort of, like, they're giving it their all, and yet they fail after an entire lifetime of trying to get the right view.

96
00:14:14,631 --> 00:14:15,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That didn't happen back then.

97
00:14:16,372 --> 00:14:24,516
[SPEAKER_00]: So that is just evidence of the fact that, you know, their minds were in a different position than ours are today.

98
00:14:25,557 --> 00:14:29,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So that process of a few minutes of listening to the Buddha,

99
00:14:30,557 --> 00:14:46,828
[SPEAKER_00]: For you to get to that point where your mind is truly dispassionate towards sensuality, you're going to have to actually give up that value and most especially the ways in which you are manifesting that value in the world.

100
00:14:47,028 --> 00:14:58,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the actual actions through bodies and speech that are making it clear that you still value sensuality as a potential solution to the issue of suffering.

101
00:15:03,129 --> 00:15:13,798
[SPEAKER_00]: In essence, that training is the gradual training that we often talk about.

102
00:15:14,278 --> 00:15:20,864
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, virtue, sense restraint, moderation, eating, watchfulness of your intentions, and all that stuff.

103
00:15:23,926 --> 00:15:28,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially, the reason why we say you need to take up that gradual training

104
00:15:34,927 --> 00:15:40,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Precisely, the main characteristic of the state of vipassana is that you don't even see what the problem is.

105
00:15:40,490 --> 00:15:43,191
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even see the size of your ignorance, to put it that way.

106
00:15:43,291 --> 00:15:54,256
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you decide or say to yourself or justify giving up only a partial amount, there's like a 99.99999% chance that

107
00:15:58,277 --> 00:16:08,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That what you're not giving up is exactly what you needed to give up in order to truly purify your mind from all these obstructions.

108
00:16:09,313 --> 00:16:13,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another... so that the Buddha said it's like... it's like a quail.

109
00:16:14,970 --> 00:16:19,874
[SPEAKER_00]: who is tied with a... with a... with, like, a rotten rope, and it says, well, this is fine.

110
00:16:19,894 --> 00:16:20,935
[SPEAKER_00]: This is just a rotten rope.

111
00:16:20,975 --> 00:16:21,255
[SPEAKER_00]: I can...

112
00:16:21,375 --> 00:16:23,257
[SPEAKER_00]: I can break out of it at any time.

113
00:16:23,817 --> 00:16:26,259
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it's a quail, it can't, actually.

114
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:29,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though, technically, it's a weak rope, it can't break out of it.

115
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:33,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like when people think, oh, this is fine.

116
00:16:33,422 --> 00:16:35,943
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not actually attached to all these things.

117
00:16:36,143 --> 00:16:46,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if that were the case, you wouldn't be so insistent on the fact that... and you wouldn't get so defensive when people tell you that you need to give it up.

118
00:16:47,349 --> 00:16:55,714
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, if you're truly confident that giving up sensuality is for your welfare, which is the direction of nibbāna in general, giving up of craving,

119
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:03,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the confidence that a stream-enterer has, you wouldn't have any problem with at least the idea that you need to give it up.

120
00:17:04,123 --> 00:17:09,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You would only not give it up if you accept that at the moment you're not practicing.

121
00:17:10,325 --> 00:17:19,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one thing that people fail to see as well in that sutta of the layman Sarakāṇī, who was drinking as a stream-enterer.

122
00:17:20,148 --> 00:17:25,070
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said at the very end of the sutta that this man undertook the training

123
00:17:26,063 --> 00:17:36,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the the the key there is, he undertook the training at the time of death, because he was not training before that.

124
00:17:37,030 --> 00:17:41,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even though he was he was a Shri Mandirer, he had understood the dhamma,

125
00:17:42,032 --> 00:17:47,697
[SPEAKER_00]: He had the perfect right meditation, as opposed to, you know, what people do today.

126
00:17:48,858 --> 00:17:56,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Even he was not able to have that right view and that right understanding, right effort, right composure, all these things, apart from his actual behavior.

127
00:17:56,944 --> 00:18:01,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, giving up the restraint meant giving up the training, even for him.

128
00:18:02,089 --> 00:18:08,414
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha said, only at the time of death, when he didn't have the chance to be careless anymore, he undertook the training again.

129
00:18:09,673 --> 00:18:16,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then to be said of somebody who doesn't even have those wholesome qualities, those wholesome factors?

130
00:18:17,297 --> 00:18:20,919
[SPEAKER_00]: And they allow themselves to cultivate the opposite of them.

131
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:24,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's just...

132
00:18:24,561 --> 00:18:26,842
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's shooting oneself in the foot, ultimately.

133
00:18:27,683 --> 00:18:37,008
[SPEAKER_00]: And the other thing to recognize as well is that the main characteristic of a stream-enterer or any noble disciple

134
00:18:37,916 --> 00:18:46,281
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha listed the five faculties, and the first two of them are a sense of shame and fear of wrongdoing, hīrīyatapa in Pāli.

135
00:18:47,081 --> 00:18:51,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And what that means is not just towards random stuff, but towards unwholesome states.

136
00:18:51,764 --> 00:18:52,705
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the sutta says.

137
00:18:52,765 --> 00:18:57,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they have a sense of shame and fear of wrongdoing in regard to unwholesome states.

138
00:18:57,888 --> 00:18:58,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

139
00:18:59,353 --> 00:19:16,915
[SPEAKER_00]: That is part of that big gap that I was talking about between when somebody who has the right view engages with with sensual pleasures and distractions and somebody who doesn't, is that that fundamental sense of shame is... or the lack of that sense of shame, because they don't they don't know unwholesome as unwholesome fully,

140
00:19:17,415 --> 00:19:19,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, as I said, the view is the most pernicious thing of all.

141
00:19:19,556 --> 00:19:23,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the puthujjana is doing it, there is a view there that's not present.

142
00:19:45,479 --> 00:19:53,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the external actions are secondary to the fundamental view, and that view is abandoned only by getting the right view.

143
00:19:54,221 --> 00:20:12,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even though you might see a stream enter externally, seemingly doing the same stuff, internally, it's not the same, because if they were to actually, like, just stop for a moment and reflect on what they're actually doing, and just snap out of it, they would immediately recognize that what they're doing is wrong.

144
00:20:12,606 --> 00:20:14,667
[SPEAKER_00]: They would be... it would be impossible to have that

145
00:20:15,223 --> 00:20:20,167
[SPEAKER_00]: That justification of, no, look, I'm a layman, it's fine, you know, it's... this is what we do.

146
00:20:20,307 --> 00:20:22,229
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it would be impossible for them to think that.

147
00:20:23,270 --> 00:20:29,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the... that's what needs to be seen.

148
00:20:29,195 --> 00:20:34,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as a bhutujjana, one doesn't... one has to tell oneself, I don't know.

149
00:20:36,039 --> 00:20:43,865
[SPEAKER_00]: What it even means to be free temporarily, free from sensual desire, which is what those people had at that moment.

150
00:20:44,666 --> 00:20:55,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I had reached that state at any point in my practice, with all the amount of knowledge that we have, and teaching that we've heard, I would have gotten the right view by that time.

151
00:20:55,435 --> 00:21:04,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to recognize that, like, if you really had gone to the point where those people were,

152
00:21:05,627 --> 00:21:15,070
[SPEAKER_00]: You already got like a thousand times more instruction that they did, so you should have the right view, if at any point in time your mind was truly dispassionate towards sensuality.

153
00:21:15,691 --> 00:21:30,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, this is one of the biggest pieces of evidence to show that whatever people usually regard today as jhāna is not actually the real jhāna that the Buddha was teaching, because the actual jhāna is already way beyond the level of development that these people had.

154
00:21:32,709 --> 00:21:34,470
[SPEAKER_00]: After which, you know, they understood the dhamma.

155
00:21:35,430 --> 00:21:44,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person today has supposedly been getting jhānas, they should be way beyond those people back in the day.

156
00:21:44,353 --> 00:21:59,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that just goes to show that it's not even actually taking you beyond the hindrances, which would have to result in seeing the Four Noble Truths, if you have heard of them before, and in such copious amounts as we have today.

157
00:22:01,788 --> 00:22:13,331
[SPEAKER_00]: You also see in the suttas that the lay people who were stream-enters and were still not fully celibate and so on, they were not getting jhānas.

158
00:22:13,812 --> 00:22:25,115
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes to show that, you know, if it really were a matter of just applying some technique, then the stream-enters should be able to do it very easily.

159
00:22:26,260 --> 00:22:29,664
[SPEAKER_00]: But they weren't getting the right... they weren't getting jhānas.

160
00:22:30,405 --> 00:22:37,332
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see which lay people were getting jhānas, it's the ones who were non-returners.

161
00:22:38,413 --> 00:22:42,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And you might think, well, they got the jhānas first, and then...

162
00:22:42,983 --> 00:22:47,667
[SPEAKER_00]: They became non-returners and then they became celibate, but it's actually the other way around.

163
00:22:48,187 --> 00:22:54,892
[SPEAKER_00]: They got the jhānas because they were celibate, and that and the jhānas made them non-returners.

164
00:22:56,173 --> 00:23:05,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... the the eh... the... that one needs to know that... recognize that one doesn't even know what that state means.

165
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:11,245
[SPEAKER_00]: So... or what what what that eh... what that eh...

166
00:23:12,444 --> 00:23:16,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Confidence in renunciation, which is a phrase that the Buddha used, what that even means.

167
00:23:16,686 --> 00:23:37,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any little sort of deviation from at least the idea you have, which is inaccurate, but it's all you have, any willing, like, volitional deviation from renunciation entails the risk that you will never reach that state.

168
00:23:37,953 --> 00:23:38,794
[SPEAKER_00]: It's as simple as that.

169
00:23:39,134 --> 00:23:39,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

170
00:23:40,848 --> 00:24:02,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could pretty much say that your situation today, building up the gradual training in the course of years, by allowing yourself to delight and engage with sensuality, and justify to yourself, is essentially the same as back in the day, when those people were talking to the Buddha,

171
00:24:04,436 --> 00:24:16,044
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were to allow themselves to have sensual thoughts, to regard as pleasant and agreeable, that which he was saying right then and there, it's unsatisfactory and unbeneficial.

172
00:24:17,205 --> 00:24:25,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in both cases, the result would be that there would be no penetrative insight of the Four Noble Truths, despite the fact that it's the same teaching that you were hearing.

173
00:24:27,673 --> 00:24:32,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will reach that state through, actually, you know, as we say,

174
00:24:33,572 --> 00:24:34,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing gradual training.

175
00:24:34,933 --> 00:24:41,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's emphatically not a result of a meditation technique, of a meditation retreat.

176
00:24:41,977 --> 00:24:50,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, for example, you can go on a 10-day retreat, and practice as hard as you can, and be celibate, and keep, you know, 300 precepts during that time.

177
00:24:51,723 --> 00:25:00,868
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if at any point in the future, your mind is still looking forward to sensuality, then the hindrance of

178
00:25:01,588 --> 00:25:03,850
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual hindrance of sensuality is not abandoned.

179
00:25:04,670 --> 00:25:10,154
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... it doesn't... it doesn't... that's why we say it has to be taken for life.

180
00:25:10,915 --> 00:25:13,317
[SPEAKER_00]: For life, I will not value sensuality.

181
00:25:13,957 --> 00:25:19,141
[SPEAKER_00]: I will give up the idea that sensuality is worthwhile for the rest of my life.

182
00:25:20,096 --> 00:25:25,719
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on the basis of that, which you... like, that idea is not just abstract.

183
00:25:25,739 --> 00:25:31,082
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to factually be implementing it, and really showing that you actually believe that through your actions.

184
00:25:31,843 --> 00:25:39,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once that's fully... once that's fulfilled, and your life has been gone, like, in concordance with that view,

185
00:25:40,380 --> 00:25:43,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can actually have a basis.

186
00:25:43,502 --> 00:25:49,365
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have the dry sticks that might just be able to give rise to the spark of knowledge.

187
00:25:49,946 --> 00:25:56,109
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, after that, once you have the right view, you can relax and you can go back to sensuality.

188
00:25:56,449 --> 00:26:06,275
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will realize that it's a completely different thing than any sort of, like, essential engagement that you could have had in the past.

189
00:26:06,435 --> 00:26:09,257
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's just... you can't even...

190
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:24,324
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even fathom the difference, as a pūtujana, what it means to be... to have the right view on one hand and value nibbāna and still be engaging with sensuality.

191
00:26:24,824 --> 00:26:28,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything you have is experienced from the point of view of the wrong view.

192
00:26:28,085 --> 00:26:35,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't magically step out of it for a moment and imagine how things are for a noble disciple.

193
00:26:37,926 --> 00:26:41,767
[SPEAKER_00]: That's essentially how it works.

194
00:26:43,048 --> 00:26:51,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thing that you need to think about is, what is the reason for you not renouncing things?

195
00:26:52,312 --> 00:26:58,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, if you have dependents, if you have a wife that depends on your children,

196
00:26:59,408 --> 00:27:08,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, aging parents, then, yeah, it would be irresponsible to, you know, leave them and potentially make them have a hard time.

197
00:27:09,373 --> 00:27:17,798
[SPEAKER_00]: But, does that mean that you need to engage in sexual intercourse in order to take care of your dependents?

198
00:27:17,898 --> 00:27:27,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it mean you need to have, you know, you need to work extra to get more wealth than is really necessary

199
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:39,222
[SPEAKER_00]: To take care of those people, do you need to be seeking out pleasant sights, sounds, smells, tastes and touches that are just for the sake of gratification?

200
00:27:39,382 --> 00:27:39,562
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

201
00:27:40,022 --> 00:27:43,703
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to do any of these things.

202
00:27:44,083 --> 00:27:54,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But people often conflate the idea of, yes, I can't become a monk with, oh, okay, so it's fine to do all the other stuff that lay people usually do.

203
00:27:54,985 --> 00:27:55,765
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not the case.

204
00:27:55,845 --> 00:27:56,045
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

205
00:27:56,969 --> 00:28:11,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though, again, like, many of those people in the suttas, you could argue, you know, they were... they didn't ordain, and they were also having sexual intercourse or whatever, the point is, again, they were Sri Mentors.

206
00:28:11,366 --> 00:28:13,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a very different thing going on there.

207
00:28:13,509 --> 00:28:15,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And lastly, it's important to note that

208
00:28:16,501 --> 00:28:23,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning sensuality is not necessary because it's... otherwise you're violating some sort of commandment or sacred rule.

209
00:28:24,587 --> 00:28:29,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because it's part and parcel of the same path towards attaining one's own welfare.

210
00:28:30,071 --> 00:28:36,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if abandoning sensuality was not for the sake of one's own welfare, the Buddha wouldn't have instructed anyone to do it.

211
00:28:38,438 --> 00:28:44,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what will happen if you're not willing to take on trust that abandoning sensuality is for... for your welfare,

212
00:28:44,877 --> 00:28:59,650
[SPEAKER_00]: You will instead take up the first, you know, watered-down version of the dhamma that allows you to... seemingly offers you the same thing, or so you think,

213
00:29:00,770 --> 00:29:10,315
[SPEAKER_00]: While at the same time allowing you to engage with those very things that are obstructing true insight from taking place.

214
00:29:11,176 --> 00:29:20,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the results of that are going to be, you know, not the actual fundamental freedom that the Buddha was teaching.

215
00:29:20,781 --> 00:29:29,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Like he said, it's the suffering that's left for a stream-enterer is like seven grains of sand compared to the great mountain that was there before.

216
00:29:30,827 --> 00:29:38,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just going to be some sort of management method that might make you feel like there are no grains of sand left at the moment.

217
00:29:39,494 --> 00:29:46,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But... so seemingly better, but in the end, it's only because you're ignoring that there is a great mountain behind you.

218
00:29:47,241 --> 00:29:53,006
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it always revolves around all that focusing and concentration.

219
00:29:53,786 --> 00:29:58,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring the full truth, pretty much, in order to feel a relief.

220
00:29:59,006 --> 00:30:18,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's in fact precisely because the actual understanding of the dhamma frees you completely to the point where suffering can't even arise, even if you want it to arise, that these people were able to pretty much give up the training, like completely just

221
00:30:21,655 --> 00:30:27,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Get careless on the surface and go on with our lives as if nothing happened.

222
00:30:27,459 --> 00:30:32,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's actually like applying to that core of your existence.

223
00:30:32,783 --> 00:30:43,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas, you know, what people teach today is just a management method that's gonna wear away within a few weeks if you stop doing it.

224
00:30:44,973 --> 00:30:47,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why people are always afraid of giving it up.

225
00:30:48,671 --> 00:30:53,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's just reinforcing that clinging to virtue and practices.

226
00:30:54,026 --> 00:30:56,252
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the end, the results will speak for themselves.

